WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.139
API keys should be transmitted only over HTTPS in production.

2
00:00:04.139 --> 00:00:12.825
Transport security helps protect credential-bearing requests in transit and helps clients communicate with the intended service.

3
00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:19.408
If a request contains a key, the route between client and service should be treated as sensitive.

4
00:00:19.408 --> 00:00:25.176
Dedicated request headers are usually easier to handle safely than query-string keys.

5
00:00:25.176 --> 00:00:31.283
Headers can be targeted by authentication handlers, gateway rules, and redaction policies.

6
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:41.123
Query strings are part of URLs, and URLs are copied, cached, logged, bookmarked, screenshotted, and sent through many tools in normal operations.

7
00:00:41.123 --> 00:00:44.652
Query-string keys create unnecessary exposure paths.

8
00:00:44.652 --> 00:00:57.070
URLs may appear in browser history, server logs, reverse proxy logs, referrer headers, analytics tools, support tickets, copied links, crash reports, sample requests, and screenshots.

9
00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:03.245
Even when the application itself is careful, surrounding tooling may preserve the full URL.

10
00:01:03.245 --> 00:01:14.917
Keys should not be placed in frontend JavaScript, public HTML, mobile app assets, downloadable configuration, or public repositories when they grant server-side privileges.

11
00:01:14.917 --> 00:01:21.703
Anything delivered to users or packaged into public assets should be assumed visible to those users.

12
00:01:21.703 --> 00:01:29.303
Diagnostics, traces, crash dumps, screenshots, and sample requests should be treated as possible exposure paths.

13
00:01:29.303 --> 00:01:35.207
A key can leave the intended trust boundary through ordinary troubleshooting artifacts.

14
00:01:35.207 --> 00:01:40.229
That is why redaction and support workflows need review before production.

15
00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:46.268
Do not log full request headers, URLs, query strings, or bodies without redaction review.

16
00:01:46.268 --> 00:01:53.190
Logs should help operators understand behavior while avoiding full credentials and sensitive payloads.

17
00:01:53.190 --> 00:02:00.858
The safest log design records identifiers, outcomes, and correlation context rather than raw credential material.
